INT. ABANDONED DRIVE-IN CONCESSION STAND - NIGHT

Dead popcorn machine. A counter thick with dust. Through the broken window, a giant blank screen glows faintly in the moonlight, its white surface pocked with rain stains.

CASS WHITLOCK (20s, flashlight, hoodie) crouches behind the counter, sorting through a box of old tickets. DRE FONTAINE (20s, camera around neck) films the room. PILAR NAVARRO (20s, sweater, scared but trying not to be) holds a thermos with both hands. WENDELL OKORO (30s, backpack, glasses) sits on an upturned bucket, reading from his phone.

CASS
Nothing in here but mice.

DRE
Mice are content. Keep rolling.

WENDELL
According to the blog, a projectionist died in 1974. Burned alive in the booth.

PILAR
Can we not?

WENDELL
I'm just reading.

DRE
Read quieter.

A SOUND outside. A thin, rhythmic scrape, like a shovel on gravel.

All four freeze.

PILAR
Was that you?

CASS
I'm right here.

DRE
Somebody's out there.

WENDELL
Maybe a groundskeeper.

PILAR
Nobody's kept this ground in thirty years.

The scraping stops. The door handle turns slowly. Then the door swings open on its own.

A STRANGER stands in the doorway. Soaked coat, no hat. His face hidden in the dark.

STRANGER
You shouldn't be here.

CASS
Sir, we're just--

STRANGER
You shouldn't be here.

DRE
(lowering the camera)
Dude, you scared the hell out of us.

PILAR
We'll leave.

WENDELL
We were leaving. We were literally--

STRANGER
It's not you I worry about.

Dre's flashlight finds his face. Gaunt, kind, tired. Not at all what they expected.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
My name is Elias. I keep the screen.

PILAR
There's a screen?

ELIAS
There's always a screen. And at midnight it plays.

WENDELL
Plays what?

ELIAS
Whatever you're most afraid of.

Cass laughs, nervous. Dre raises the camera again.

DRE
Okay, that's good. Do that again.

ELIAS
It's ten to midnight.

Pilar grabs Cass's sleeve. Wendell closes his phone. Beyond the window, the giant screen flickers from white to grey to black.

PILAR
(whisper)
Cass, I want to go.

CASS
We're going.

WENDELL
Which way?

ELIAS
Don't take the road. Take the field.

DRE
Why the field?

ELIAS
Because the road is where it waits.

The first frame of film lights the screen. Four faces turn toward it, orange and bright.

On the screen: the concession stand. Four silhouettes in the window. And behind them, a fifth.
